  "id": "DrugBank_DB00792 - MONDO_5492",
  "verbalization": "Tripelennamine is implicated in the pathogenesis of urticaria through a series of pharmacological pathways that involve angioedema. The evidence suggests that Tripelennamine may act as a mediator in the development of angioedema, which in turn could contribute to the onset of urticaria. This is supported by the indication of Tripelennamine for angioedema, which is a precursor condition to urticaria, as indicated by the multiple paths linking Tripelennamine to angioedema and then to urticaria. The shared indication of angioedema in all three paths points to a potential common mechanism, possibly involving the modulation of inflammatory pathways or mast cell activation. The presence of multiple pathways converging on urticaria suggests a possible role of Tripelennamine in the inflammatory cascade that underlies urticaria. While the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated, the integrated evidence supports a mechanistic link between Tripelennamine and urticaria, particularly through its involvement in angioedema.",
